# goose — Local-First AI Agent Runtime

## What it is

goose is a **general-purpose AI agent that runs entirely on your machine** — not cloud-dependent, not editor-locked. It ships as a native desktop app, full CLI, and embeddable API, built in Rust for performance and portability.

**Now hosted at the [Agentic AI Foundation (AAIF)](https://aaif.io/)** under the Linux Foundation, alongside MCP and AGENTS.md. This ensures neutral, community-governed governance.

**Latest version**: v1.37 (June 2026), 19,740+ GitHub stars, Apache-2.0 license.

## Core surfaces

| Surface | Use case |
|---|---|
| **Desktop app** | Visual workspace, MCP app UIs, session management (macOS/Linux/Windows) |
| **CLI** | Headless automation, CI/CD integration, scripted workflows |
| **API** | Embed goose into custom systems, trigger from webhooks or job queues |

## Core innovations (v1.36/v1.37)

### 1. YAML Recipes — Agent behavior as code

Recipes are declarative YAML files that package the complete agent configuration:
- **Instructions**: system prompt + behavior definition
- **Extensions**: which MCP tools the agent can use
- **Parameters**: Jinja2-templated, user-configurable inputs
- **Settings**: model provider, temperature, max_turns
- **Sub-recipes**: compose complex workflows from smaller recipes
- **Success validation**: shell checks, retry logic

```yaml
# recipes/scope.yaml
version: "1.0.0"
title: "Scope an issue"
description: "Read the issue and the repo, produce an implementation plan"
settings:
  goose_provider: anthropic
  goose_model: claude-opus-5
extensions:
  - type: builtin
    name: developer
parameters:
  - key: context_path
    input_type: string
    requirement: required
    description: "Path to the rendered issue context file"
instructions: |
  You are scoping, not building. Do not modify tracked files.
prompt: |
  Read {{ context_path }} and the repository, then write a plan.
```

Key insight: **Recipe changes are git diffs** — agent behavior is code-reviewed like any other code. Swapping models is a one-line change. The entire behavior lives in the repo.

Sub-recipes enable sequential/parallel orchestration:
```yaml
sub_recipes:
  - name: analyze_frontend
    path: "./sub-recipes/frontend-analyzer.yaml"
    description: "Analyze React components"
    values:
      severity: "high"
  - name: security_scan
    path: "./sub-recipes/security-scanner.yaml"
    sequential_when_repeated: true  # Don't run in parallel
```

### 2. PreToolUse + Stop Hooks — Governance layer

v1.36 introduced `PreToolUse` denial hooks to intercept and block tool calls before execution.
v1.37 added blocking `Stop` hooks. Combined with read-only mounts and path checks, this creates a real enforcement boundary outside the agent itself.

### 3. 70+ MCP Extensions

MCP is goose's native integration protocol. Extensions can render **interactive UIs inside goose Desktop** — buttons, forms, visualizations — enabling agent-powered tools without custom UI code.

### 4. ACP (Agent Client Protocol)

Separates the agent runtime from the interface. goose can be controlled from a terminal, Slack bot, IDE plugin, or custom dashboard via a standard protocol. Clients can send per-session system prompts without interfering with each other.

### 5. Declarative YAML Providers

New in v1.36/v1.37: many providers are now configured entirely in YAML, making provider management infrastructure-as-code.

**9 new providers added**: Alibaba Qwen/DashScope, Perplexity, Databricks AI Gateway, Vercel AI Gateway, xAI SuperGrok, Scaleway, NEAR AI Cloud, oMLX, GitHub Copilot `/responses`.

### 6. Local inference improvements

- Linux Vulkan acceleration
- Alpine/musl builds for minimal environments
- Windows CUDA artifacts
- Native arm64 Linux runners
- Strict GGUF model validation

## When to use goose

| Use goose when you need | Use alternatives when |
|---|---|
| Local, open-source agent runtime | Cloud-managed agent (Claude Code, Codex) |
| Model/provider flexibility | First-party model integration |
| YAML-defined agent behaviors (git-reviewable) | Dashboard-based agent config |
| Headless containerized runs | Interactive IDE-first workflows |
| MCP extension portability | Native plugin ecosystem |
| Read-only mount enforcement | Full filesystem access |
| Self-hosted, no cloud dependency | Managed cloud control plane |

